================================================================================
Third-party components
================================================================================

No third-party CODE is vendored into this repository. Five third-party FONT FILES
are: three under `bins/pl-gui/fonts/` -- two since 2026-07-30 and Inter SemiBold
since 2026-08-09 -- for the interface, and two under `crates/pl-draw/fonts/`
since 2026-08-03, for the PNG raster back end.
They are the first five entries below. Eight third-party LICENCE TEXTS are
committed too -- one covering the two Plex faces, one covering the two Liberation
faces, one covering Inter, and one for each of the five that arrive through a
crate -- because four of those licences require their text to accompany every
copy, and a path into a Cargo registry is not something a recipient of `dist/`
has.

One third-party TEST-VECTOR FILE is vendored as well, since 2026-08-05:
`crates/pl-core/vectors/ed25519_wycheproof.tsv`, under "Data" below. It is
Apache-2.0, the same licence as this repository, and it is test data only --
`#[cfg(test)]` reads it, so it is in no shipped binary.

The two directories are separate on purpose. `bins/pl-gui/fonts/` is what the
interface draws with; `crates/pl-draw/fonts/` is what the raster exporter fills
outlines from, and it lives in the crate because `bins/pl` needs it too. Nothing
uses a face from the other directory.

Fonts embedded in the SHIPPED BINARY
------------------------------------

Ten faces, 3,387,456 bytes, in polylinker.exe. Five are checked in here and
five arrive through a crate.

825,168 of those bytes -- the two Liberation faces, and nothing else in this
list -- are in pl.exe as well, which reaches the same code through
`pl export --png`. They are the only font data in that binary.

THIS PARAGRAPH READ "Seven faces, 2,282,880 bytes ... Two are checked in here"
FOR ONE DAY, 2026-08-03 to 2026-08-04, and the arithmetic is what pinned which
seven it meant: 373,552 + 1,414,020 + 495,308 = 2,282,880 exactly, so the two
Liberation faces added the day before were the ones missing. Both shipped
executables link them -- the app through its export menu and `pl` through
`--png`, each of which calls `pl_draw::png_at`, which rasterizes text by filling
outlines from `crates/pl-draw/src/font.rs`. The top of this section already said
four font files are vendored, so the file contradicted itself. It is now summed
from the entries below by
`the_face_count_notice_states_is_the_faces_notice_lists`, which reads the two
committed pairs' lengths off the constants that embed them.

What the two Plex faces cost, measured rather than estimated: a release
build of `bins/pl-gui` went from 8,682,496 bytes at 0aa0f88 to 9,082,368 with
them in, +399,872 (+4.61%). The faces themselves are 373,552 of that; the
remaining 26,320 is what `lto = true` and `strip = true` did with the rest of
the change and is not attributable to the fonts alone. Font bytes reach `.rdata`
verbatim -- link-time optimisation does not compress them -- which is why they
are close to 1:1 on the executable and why the subsetting question below was
worth answering rather than assuming.

The two Plex faces came in with the 2026-07-30 typography pass and the two
Liberation faces with the PNG back end on 2026-08-03. Everything
about them below was read from the bytes committed to this repository -- the
version from the file's own `name` table, the advance from its `hmtx`, the
licence from the `LICENSE.txt` in the same release archive -- and not from a
changelog, a README, or the repository badge on the upstream project. That
distinction is deliberate: `features/SOURCING.md` records the Sequence Ontology
entry being overturned because a machine-generated LICENSE restating a badge was
mistaken for independent corroboration, and a GitHub `spdx_id` is the same class
of artefact. api.github.com reports IBM/plex as OFL-1.1, and that is recorded
here as corroboration only.

TWO VERSION NUMBERS PER FACE, AND THEY DISAGREE ON PURPOSE. The npm package tag
and the typeface version are independent counters -- `@ibm/plex-mono@2.5.0` ships
a font whose `name` ID 5 reads "Version 2.005". Only the typeface version can be
read back out of the shipped .exe, so a record carrying only the tag cannot be
checked against the binary.

The sha256 of each committed file is the one a third party can verify against
IBM's own release archive, because neither face is subsetted or re-encoded. See
"Why neither Plex face is subsetted" below.

