  Why this copy is a Modified Version, and why that is allowed:
    This copy maps NO Private-Use-Area codepoints at all -- counted out of its
    own cmap: 2,104 codepoints, none of them in U+E000..U+F8FF. (The vendoring
    project's own script puts upstream Inter's PUA coverage at roughly 745
    codepoints; that figure is repeated as its claim and was not re-measured
    here, no unmodified copy being available to count.)
    They are absent because the project it was vendored from subsets them out,
    because there Inter
    sits at the head of the proportional chain and would otherwise shadow the
    Phosphor icon face, whose glyphs live in the same range. Polylinker does not
    need that -- Phosphor is in a family of its own here and nothing can shadow
    it -- but the stripped file is strictly safer and no unmodified copy was
    available to vendor instead.
    OFL 1.1 makes a subset a Modified Version, since it "deletes components of
    the Original Version". Clause 3 then forbids a Modified Version carrying a
    RESERVED FONT NAME, and Inter declares none -- its copyright line is bare,
    where IBM Plex's reads `with Reserved Font Name "Plex"`. That is why the two
    faces are treated differently in this file: neither Plex face may be
    subsetted without a rename, and this one may keep its name. Both licence
    texts are committed, so a recipient can check the claim.
    THE CONSEQUENCE FOR PROVENANCE IS THAT THE SHA256 ABOVE MATCHES NOTHING
    UPSTREAM. It pins these bytes against silent replacement and nothing more,
    which is the same position Phosphor is in below and is stated here for the
    same reason. To reproduce the file: take Inter 4.001 Regular's SemiBold
    weight and run fontTools' Subsetter over it keeping every codepoint outside
    U+E000-U+F8FF, with all OpenType layout features, glyph names and the full
    name table retained.

  Why Inter is not the proportional face, only the heading one:
    The design system this was ported from puts Inter Regular at the head of the
    proportional chain. That half was refused, and the reason is measurable
    rather than aesthetic. Read out of the `glyf` bounding boxes, in em:

               capital I   lowercase l   ratio
      Plex Sans   0.2800       0.1560     1.79
      Inter Reg   0.0928       0.0879     1.06
      Inter SemiB 0.1304       0.1270     1.03

    Inter draws both as the same bare vertical stroke. This application's
    proportional text is enzyme names -- `HindIII`, `SfiI`, `AflII`,
    `BspLU11III` -- and each has a differently-named lookalike spelled with
    `l`. Mis-reading one is a wrong digest, so this is legibility as
    correctness, and it is the same defect Plex Sans was vendored to fix in the
    first place: Ubuntu Light's `I` was a bare stem too.
    Inter Regular is therefore NOT vendored at all. With Plex Sans keeping the
    body it would be 278 KB of face that draws nothing, plus a licence
    obligation for it.
    Asserted by `the_proportional_face_tells_a_capital_i_from_a_lowercase_l`,
    which measures the rasterised ink through the real font atlas and holds the
    ratio at 1.4 -- clear of both faces by a wide margin in either direction.

