  Why embedding is permitted, on three independent grounds:
    1. OFL 1.1 PERMISSION & CONDITIONS grants the right to "embed" by name, and
       clause 2 permits Original Versions to be "bundled, redistributed and/or
       sold with any software, provided that each copy contains the above
       copyright notice and this license". CLAUSE 2 IS WHAT LETS AN APACHE-2.0
       EXECUTABLE CARRY AN OFL FACE, and the condition attached to it -- that
       the notice and the licence travel with each copy -- is what
       `tools/release.ps1` exists to satisfy. Clause 1 forbids only selling the
       Font Software BY ITSELF, which this project does not do and does not
       sell at all.
       A SEPARATE QUESTION, ANSWERED BY A SEPARATE CLAUSE, and the two were
       conflated here for one review cycle: clause 5 keeps the FONT under OFL
       and carves out "any document created using the Font Software" -- that
       is the .dna and .gb files Polylinker writes, which are not OFL and do
       not become OFL. It says nothing about an executable that embeds the
       font file, because an executable is not a document created using it.
       Clause 2 does that work. The conclusion is unchanged; the reasoning was
       wrong and this paragraph is the project's stated position, so it is the
       kind of thing that gets quoted back later.
    2. OS/2 fsType = 0 in both faces: Installable Embedding, the least
       restrictive value the specification defines. The font's own
       machine-readable permission, agreeing with its licence.
    3. OFL is already on this project's allow-list for embedded fonts -- Noto
       Emoji below is recorded under it and accepted without a question flag.
       These two faces needed no new policy decision.

  Why neither Plex face is subsetted, which is the one real constraint:
    The Reserved Font Name is "Plex". OFL 1.1 defines a Modified Version as any
    derivative made by "adding to, deleting, or substituting -- in part or in
    whole -- any of the components of the Original Version". A subset DELETES
    components, so a subset is a Modified Version, and clause 3 then forbids it
    carrying "Plex" as its primary font name. Subsetting would therefore cost a
    rename, and NOTICE would then record a sha256 and an upstream URL for a file
    that exists nowhere upstream -- which defeats the purpose of recording
    either. 373,552 bytes of unmodified, independently verifiable face is a
    better trade than roughly 60 KB and a broken provenance chain.
    NOTHING HERE IS LEGAL ADVICE. This is the item to put to counsel if anyone
    ever proposes subsetting a Plex face.

