Data
================================================================================

Restriction-enzyme recognition sequences appearing in
prototype/dna-reader.html and reference/python/tests/validate_digest.py were
transcribed independently from standard published references. They are
statements of biological fact about approximately fifty well-known Type II
enzymes, not a reproduction of any database.

Production enzyme data will be sourced from REBASE (Roberts lab, New England
Biolabs) and packaged in a SEPARATE repository under REBASE's own terms with
its own NOTICE. REBASE data is not Apache-2.0 and must not be commingled with
this repository's code.

  REBASE                http://rebase.neb.com/
  Citation              Roberts RJ, Vincze T, Posfai J, Macelis D.
                        REBASE: a database for DNA restriction and modification.
                        Nucleic Acids Research.

The annotation database (polylinker-features) is CC BY 4.0. It is IN THIS
REPOSITORY, in `features/`, and `crates/pl-features` compiles both
`features/features.tsv` and `features/provenance.tsv` into every binary via
`include_str!` -- so `pl`, `pl-mcp` and the Python module all carry it and all
carry its attribution obligations. `pl licences` prints this section from the
compiled-in table.

Sources actually in use, with the licence recorded per FIELD in
`features/provenance.tsv` (a single record legitimately mixes several):

  NCBI AMRFinderPlus       INSDC free-and-unrestricted, NLM attribution
  UniProt Consortium       CC BY 4.0, (c) 2002-2024 UniProt Consortium
  EMBL-EBI / ENA           INSDC free-and-unrestricted, credit to submitters
  EMBL-EBI / Rfam          CC0 1.0, with per-family primary-source credit
  INSDC feature table      licence UNRESOLVED; see SOURCING.md Risk 4

  Courtesy of the U.S. National Library of Medicine.
  https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/home/about/policies/

  This dataset is a dated snapshot (retrieved 2026-07-28) and does not reflect
  the most current data available from NLM.

FPbase and NCBI UniVec appear nowhere in it and must not: `features/SOURCING.md`
records FPbase as HOLD (its own page disclaims the power to grant a licence) and
UniVec as NO-GO as a coordinate source. This paragraph named both of them as
sources while the database was being built from neither.

The full notice -- the UniProt statement of changes, the per-family Rfam credit
table, and the list of sources deliberately not used -- is `features/NOTICE`,
and it must be packaged with any distribution that includes the database.

The planned correctness benchmark (polylinker-bench) will be CC0.

